        "id": "ch15_3_3-2",
        "text": "the storage capacity \u2013 by adding additional data disks to virtual machines. We can add existing data\ndisks or create new ones.\nLet\u2019s look at how to add a secondary data disk to our virtual machine.\nAdding additional storage\nAzure can add disks to our instance on the fly, without stopping the machine. We can add two types of\ndisks to a virtual machine: data disks and managed disks . In this chapter, we will only provide information\non how to add data disks. For more information on disk types on Azure, please visit https://docs.\nmicrosoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/disks-types .\nLet\u2019s learn how to add a data disk:\n1.\nNavigate to Virtual machines in the left navigation menu and select your instance, click Disks\nunder Settings , and then click on Create and attach a new disk .\n2.\nIn the disk properties, leave the Logical Unit Number ( LUN ) as-is (automatically assigned)\nand specify Disk name (such as packt-disk ), Storage Type , and Size (such as 4 GB ) values.\nClick Apply when you\u2019re done:\nWorking with Microsoft Azure\n565\nFigure 15.42 \u2013 Adding a new data disk\nAt this point, the new disk is attached to our virtual machine, but the disk hasn\u2019t been initialized\nwith a filesystem yet.\n3.\nConnect to our virtual machine via SSH:\nssh -i packt-rhel.pem packt@20.19.173.232\n4.\nList the current block devices:\nlsblk\nDeploying to the Cloud with AWS and Azure",
